  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 150 AM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe upper high will strengthen on Thursday to 598 dam as it begins\nto shift westward, though for much of the day will remain over\ncentral NM. Though storm coverage should be similar to or slightly\nless than Wednesday, storms will still be able to produce heavy\nrainfall as PWATs remain unchanged. Meanwhile, the vort max\nproduced by last nights convection over Mexico looks to merge with\na weak shortwave shifting over northern CA later today and move \nover the top of the ridge Thursday morning and across eastern CO \nThursday afternoon. The convection that ensues over CO, NE and KS \nwill send another outflow boundary into NE NM late Thursday night \ninto Friday. This should set the stage for additional shower and \nthunderstorm activity across eastern NM on Friday afternoon and \nevening. Meanwhile on Friday, the upper high will have shifted \nwest- northwest to the AZ/UT border and strengthened to at least \n600dam at H5. Temperatures should rise another degree or two with \ntriple digit heat returning around FMN. Saturday looks to remain \nactive with regards to precip, at least along the Central \nMountain Chain and Southwest Mountains, but drier air may start to\nmove into NW NM around the upper high. The drier air may continue\nto push more into NM early next week reducing storm coverage \nSunday and Monday. PWATs may finally dip below 1 inch.",
